WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.230
MARC COHEN: Hello,
everyone, and welcome.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:13.220
In this session, we're going
to build a web app together.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.530
We're going to containerize it.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.980
And don't worry if you're
not sure what that means.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.570
We'll explain it.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.400
And we're going to
deploy it to the Cloud.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.700
And I think you'll see, as
we go along how easy it is.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.510
And that's one of the things I
love about our Cloud platform.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.580
My name is Marc Cohen.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.390
I'm a developer advocate.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.290
You can find my blog at mco.dev.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.395
And on Twitter, my
handle is mco_ev.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.290
And let's go to the workshop.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.340
So I have one more
link for you--

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.920
g.co/codelabs/dev2prod.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.190
So one more time, that's g.co--

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.370
g dot C-O slash codelabs--

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.690
C-O-D-E-LA-B-S-- slash
dev, number 2, prod--

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:07.680
D-E-V 2 P-R-O-D.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.350
So if you go there,
you will see something

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.200
that looks a lot like
the window on the left.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.450
And this arrangement
you see here

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:23.130
is what we're going to
use for the entire lab.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.000
What's on the left is the
codelab that you should see,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.270
and what's on the right of the
screen is the Cloud Console.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:33.360
So I'll be able to go back and
forth without changing windows

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.380
today.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.000
I invite you to set up
something similar to that.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.800
But whatever you're
comfortable with is fine.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.270
So let's get into it.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.440
First of all, I want to just
give you some background

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.770
about application deployment.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.160
And the key here
to think about is,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.920
if you have a good
application, and you

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.995
want to deploy it
nowadays, there

00:01:57.995 --> 00:01:59.370
are a ton of
different things you

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:00.995
have to worry about--
like provisioning

00:02:00.995 --> 00:02:05.520
hardware, installing
software, security patching,

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.759
the economics of it, scaling
up and down, and so on.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:10.320
It gets really complex.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.120
And most of those
things are really

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.870
having to do with
infrastructure.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:18.150
They don't have to do with your
application business logic.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:21.460
And what I really
want to concentrate

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.793
on when I'm writing an app
is just solving my problem.

00:02:23.793 --> 00:02:25.460
I don't want to do
all that other stuff.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.090
And so what I'm going to
illustrate for you today

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:31.480
is a way to let Google take
care of all the infrastructure

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.510
for you, so that you can
just focus all your attention

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:35.560
on your application.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:43.960
So let's get set up.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.080
What we're going to do
is create a project.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.890
So you're going to go
to the Cloud Console.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.872
That's console.cloud.google.com.

00:02:51.872 --> 00:02:55.060
And you're going to
create a new project.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.800
And you can do that by selecting
this pulldown up the top here

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.850
and click this New
Project button.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.520
This will prompt you for
a Project Name, a Billing

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.020
Account.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.540
And in this Billing
Account pulldown,

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.730
that's where you can
select the free trial

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:16.230
account that you just created
if you're using the credits.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:17.573
And then, you'll click Create.

00:03:17.573 --> 00:03:18.990
I'm not going to
do that right now

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.690
because I have a project I'm
going to use that I've already

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.510
set up.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.030
But that's what you want to
do if you don't have a project

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.100
you already want to use.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.840
If not, you can
reuse your project.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.240
The next step is, we're going
to start the Cloud Shell.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.570
So the Cloud Shell
is this little widget

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.000
in the upper right-hand corner.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.360
If you hover over it, it
says Activate Cloud Shell.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.880
So go ahead and click on that.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.300
And you'll get this window
or this tab popping up

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.760
at the bottom of your screen,
saying Provisioning Your Cloud

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:54.810
Shell Machine.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.170
Let me make this bigger for you.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.780
And what this is
doing is giving you

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:02.390
a login and an
interactive command

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:06.270
processor on a virtual machine
that was allocated for you.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:07.340
So it's all ready to go.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.470
And you already have
a development place

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:11.240
in the Cloud essentially.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.460
To make this more
visible, I'm going

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:19.070
to change my prompt to
give me a little more

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.780
room to type on the screen here.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:26.780
So that's your Cloud
Shell environment.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:30.140
And you're going to
probably have everything

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.000
pre-setup the way you want.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.570
But just in case,
you want to make sure

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.780
that your project is set.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:42.620
So you'll run this command
gcloud config list project.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:46.010
And you should see it displaying
the project that you've just

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.650
created or that you're using.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.360
You've got that set up.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.770
You can also say gcloud
off list as shown here.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:56.450
And that will
essentially tell you

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.400
your account is set
correctly and you

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.980
are authenticated rather.

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.210
So you want to do that as well.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:06.440
And if the project's not
set correctly, by the way,

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.555
you can do this command--

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.750
gcloud config set project.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.810
And then, type whatever
your project is.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:20.810
But you should be OK
with it by default.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:26.090
I also want you to copy/paste
these Environment Variable

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:27.380
Settings.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:32.540
This is setting the project
name in a short variable

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:33.750
that we'll use later--

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.920
the app name, the port name, a
default region, and something

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:42.020
called tag, which we'll use
when we containerize your app.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:45.440
Last thing in this
getting set up stage

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.190
is we're going to enable three
services that we need to use.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.800
So you'll paste those.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.790
And by the way, when you
hit the first command

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:57.853
in Cloud Shell that
requires authorization,

00:05:57.853 --> 00:05:59.270
you'll get this
little pop-up that

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:00.730
says Authorize Cloud Shell.

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:03.140
Go ahead and click
Authorize there.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:05.300
And we're setting up
Cloud Build, which

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.580
will enable us to build
our project in the gcloud,

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.370
Container Registry to
store our container,

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.200
and the Cloud Run service.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.930
So once you've
got those enabled,

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.982
let's go to the
next step, which is,

00:06:17.982 --> 00:06:19.190
we're going to build our app.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:22.220
Now, this is going
to be a simple app.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.220
But that's fine.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.670
We want a vehicle that's
not too complicated that we

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:27.470
can use to then
see it run locally,

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:30.570
or at least running in this
development environment.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.990
And then, we're going to
deploy it to the Cloud.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.620
So what I want you to do next
is click this Open Editor button

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.120
that I'm hovering
over right here.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.860
And what that will do is,
it will create essentially

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.770
an integrated development
environment in the same window.

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:54.350
So now you'll have a terminal,
an IDE, and mCloud Console.

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:57.590
And what I like to do here is,
if you look at this bar then

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:01.370
I'm hovering on, you can slide
it up as far as you want.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:04.400
And that gives you a little
bit more screen real estate

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.280
for your terminal and Shell.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.680
And you can go back and
forth between the editor

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:11.790
and the terminal.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:14.070
Sorry, I'm using
different terms there.

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.480
The editor and the terminal--

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.000
I'll try to use those
words consistently.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.730
You can ping pong back and
forth by clicking these buttons.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.450
And then, you always have
the Cloud Console up here.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:26.230
You can pull this down if you
need to do more work up there.

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:28.740
So I find this to be
a convenient setup

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.970
for getting things done
in one combined window.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.010
So let's build our app.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:35.720
The first thing
we're going to do

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.450
is to create a
folder for the app.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:40.340
And let's call
this folder Hello.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:45.110
So we'll pull down
File, New Folder.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.730
Enter the name Hello.

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:48.660
You can actually call
it anything you want,

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.510
but I'm going to
give it that name.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:54.210
Do a little cleanup here.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.590
Next, you're going to
want to create a new file.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:00.920
And if you do that, it'll put
it in that folder automatically.

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:03.290
I think I managed to
put Hello under Test,

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:04.940
which isn't going to bother me.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:08.390
But you'll hopefully
get the Hello folder

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:10.310
created at the top level.

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:13.400
So you're going to do
the Create New File.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:17.600
And the name of the file is
going to be requirements.text--

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.800
that's a plural,
requirements.text.

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:24.630
That's under Hello, which
is where we wanted it to be.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:26.810
We're going to put
these two words

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:30.020
in there-- flask and Gunicorn.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:33.799
Now, for Python people,
you'll know what this is.

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:36.650
But if you're new to
this, requirements.text

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:38.840
is a conventional place
to put the required

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:42.059
packages for Python programs.

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:46.640
So once we've done that,
we're going to create

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.410
the first version of our app.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:52.160
And I'm just going to copy/paste
this code under version 1,

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:53.450
Hello, World.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:56.690
I'm going to create a new file.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:09:00.950
[INAUDIBLE] main.py--
if I can type it.

00:09:03.794 --> 00:09:05.560
And we'll paste
that code in there.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.330
Let's take a real quick look.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:11.290
This is using the Flask
package, creating an app,

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:15.190
getting a port number from
the environment variable

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:16.630
we set earlier.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:20.890
This route is telling it to
support this Say Hello function

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:23.710
for any incoming
web requests that

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:27.640
are directed to the
root of the URL,

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:30.010
or the root path
you might call it,

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:33.040
and that come in with
an HTTP get request.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.140
And all this is going
to do, this function,

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:40.000
is set a variable called HTML
to this very trivial HTML

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.130
document that says
Hello, World and returns

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:45.340
that, which will essentially
get it to percolate out

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:47.720
to the web browser.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:52.660
So the next thing we're going to
do is go back to the terminal.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:56.230
And you may wonder about why
I didn't have you save it.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:10:00.460
These files will
autosave in the editor.

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:02.380
So I'm back to the terminal.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:05.410
And as I mentioned
earlier, I accidentally

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:07.855
put the Hello
directory under test.

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:24.420
And so for me, just to repeat
that, I had the CD test Hello

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:25.770
to get to the stuff I wanted.

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:27.630
For you, you should
just see Hello,

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:30.510
and you'll be able to
just say, CD Hello.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:35.280
So hopefully, I'm the only
person with that glitch.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.890
Now, I've got my
requirements.text.

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:40.560
And what I want to do is
install the requirements.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:45.240
And I'll do that by saying
tip3 install minus r

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:46.920
requirements.text.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.320
And that's already finished.

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:51.400
And next, I'll run
my example app.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.940
So with those two files, I
have a very simple web app.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:57.630
I'm going to run it by
saying python3 main.py.

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:01.360
And what I can do now is
click this Web Preview button.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:07.170
If you click there, you'll
see a preview on port 8080.

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:09.570
And if you do that, it
should take you to your app.

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:10.450
And there you go.

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:17.160
You have your very sophisticated
web app that says Hello, World.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.610
So you can hopefully see
that working for yourself.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:23.880
We're going to tweak the
app just a little bit.

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:27.120
And I'm going to actually skip
version 2 and go to version 3

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.690
because they're just
basically adding

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:33.360
a little bit of
successive functionality.

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:36.030
And I don't think there's
huge value right now in doing

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.930
each step one at a time.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:42.698
So we're going to jump to the
end of this portion of the lab.

00:11:42.698 --> 00:11:44.740
So what I'd like you to
do is go back to main.py.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:50.680
I'm going to wipe
out what I had there.

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:52.140
Now, when you do
this on your own,

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:55.470
you can actually make
the changes piecemeal

00:11:55.470 --> 00:12:00.600
because I've annotated this lab
to say this line here is new.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.930
This line is modified, so you
can see the exact changes.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:05.850
But for expedience,
I'm just going

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:10.610
to select this whole version 3.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:14.470
I'm going to paste
it into main.py.

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:17.810
And just to give you a
sense of what this is about,

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:23.180
I've modified it so that it
takes a portion of the URL.

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:24.572
And if it exists--

00:12:24.572 --> 00:12:26.030
meaning if there's
something coming

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:28.040
after the slash in the URL--

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.300
it will echo that
back instead of World.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:35.838
So if I have slash Google I/O,
it'll say Hello, Google I/O.

00:12:35.838 --> 00:12:37.880
And then, the other thing
is, it's playing around

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:42.060
with the background color for
the stuff that it presents.

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:45.230
So let's go ahead
back to the terminal.

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:48.560
If you enter Control-C
it should break out

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:53.570
of the existing app
that was running.

00:12:53.570 --> 00:13:02.015
And let's go back to run
python3 space main.py.

00:13:02.015 --> 00:13:03.890
And we'll do the same
thing we did as before.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:07.700
We'll open the
previewer to port 8080.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:13.230
And there we see the app with
a random background color.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.440
The other thing I
wanted to mention

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:18.680
is, as I said earlier, you
can change a little bit

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:21.680
after to slash to
anything you'd like,

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:25.280
and you get Hello,
Google I/O. So we've

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:26.750
tweaked the app a little bit.

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:28.400
And I hope that
gives you a sense

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:33.080
that you've got a pretty
straightforward development

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:36.230
environment with both the
terminal and an editor embedded

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:40.870
right here into
the Cloud Console.

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:42.500
So hopefully, you've
got that working.

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.060
We'll go on to the
next step, which

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:46.170
is to containerize the app.

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.670
Now, I want to take
just a minute to explain

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:51.770
what containerising means.

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:56.280
If you imagine taking this app
and sharing it with someone,

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:59.310
they would need to make sure
they had all of the things you

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:00.660
have in your environment.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:03.135
And the environment can
encompass a lot of things,

00:14:03.135 --> 00:14:04.530
right?

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:07.050
One way to improve that
problem is to give them

00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:09.330
your requirements.text.

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:11.040
But there's some issues there.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.400
For example, I didn't
specify a particular version

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:16.680
of Flask or Gunicorn.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:19.740
And let's imagine someone
installs this on their system,

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:22.580
and they have a different
version of Flask or Gunicorn,

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:24.240
or those packages change.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:28.110
And the most recent
version works differently.

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:31.230
On top of that, the operating
system might have some quirks.

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:34.470
They might be using a
different version of Python.

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:36.720
There's all sorts
of possible ways

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:40.620
that someone who takes this
code could run into problems.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:43.050
And wouldn't it be nice
if we could package up

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:45.990
everything this app needs,
all of its dependencies,

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:50.190
into one modular bubble?

00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:52.290
And that's what a
container essentially is.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:54.390
It lets you define
everything you

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:56.990
need to run this app anywhere.

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:59.960
The way you specify a
container is through something

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:01.220
called a Docker file.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:03.320
So I want you to
create a new file again

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:04.775
called Docker file
with a capital

00:15:04.775 --> 00:15:09.740
D. You're going to copy
this text into there.

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:12.710
And I'll just briefly walk
through what this is doing.

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:20.430
Think of a Docker file as a
blueprint for a container.

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:23.720
So it starts with a front line,
which says this is the base.

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:28.670
And that's a prepackaged
foundation for your container.

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:30.590
We're going to tell it
the working directory.

00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:31.830
We're going to do everything.

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:36.600
It's the home directory for
the container/app in this case.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.390
We're going to
copy our two files,

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:42.590
main.py and requirements.text.

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:45.500
We'll install the
requirements as we're

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:46.790
building the container.

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:48.810
We're going to set
the port number.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:50.960
And then, here's
the command to run

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:53.060
when you start the container.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:56.338
And with that, we can
build the container.

00:15:56.338 --> 00:15:58.130
And we're going to do
that using the Cloud.

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:00.440
There are ways to do
this locally using

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:01.400
the Docker command.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:03.680
But I'm going to switch
back to the terminal.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:08.510
Control-C out of my
running Python app.

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:15.990
And I'm going to do this gcloud
builds submit minus minus tag.

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:20.400
Now, the tag is really just
giving the resulting container

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:25.140
image a unique name
in a namespace,

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:27.300
so that you can then
refer to the container

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:32.000
later when you want to run
it in the Cloud or locally.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:35.330
So this takes a minute or so.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:37.580
But what's happening
here is, it's both

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:40.770
building the container, and
it's pushing it to a repository

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:43.230
so we can refer to it later.

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:45.440
So now we have the
container bill.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:48.350
We can say Gcloud
container images

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:52.430
list if we want to actually see
all the containers we've built.

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:55.610
And here's the one
I just made, gcr.io.

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:59.240
That's the container
repository, a registry.

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:00.470
I think.

00:17:00.470 --> 00:17:02.810
mco Cloud Run is my project.

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:05.160
And Hello is the app.

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:09.650
So that container has the app
in it, and it's ready to run.

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:11.300
Now before I go to
the Cloud, let's

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:14.150
just run that container
locally, which we can do.

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:19.310
And just test that the container
works exactly like the app

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:24.635
did when I ran it
with python3 main.py.

00:17:24.635 --> 00:17:26.869
I'm giving a couple arguments
for something called

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:30.860
port mapping, and that's to map
the port from the host system

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:35.390
from my MacBook into the
internal port in the containers

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:36.690
view of the world.

00:17:36.690 --> 00:17:38.630
And I end the command
with dollar tag.

00:17:38.630 --> 00:17:41.090
And again, that's
how I tell Docker,

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:43.790
that's the image that
I want you to run.

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:46.130
It said, unable to find
an image because it didn't

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:47.520
see a copy of it locally.

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:50.490
So it pulled it from
the Container Registry.

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:53.670
And now, it should be running--

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:59.510
although I was expecting
to see a little bit more.

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:03.130
Let's go ahead and
see if it's running.

00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:04.690
And yeah, it is running.

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:10.270
So you can see that it's
looking exactly like it

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:11.560
was when I ran it locally.

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:13.280
And just to prove
it's actually running,

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:18.970
I'm going to cancel out of
it, go back here, and refresh

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:19.470
the screen.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:23.790
And you see, I couldn't
connect to server on port 8080.

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:26.310
So just to review what we've
done-- we built an app.

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:28.350
We ran it locally.

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:29.910
And then, we containerised it.

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:32.040
And by doing so, we
now have this module

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:34.830
I can give to anyone
and be confident

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:37.410
that app is going to
run exactly for them

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.460
the same way it did for me--

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:40.470
which is super powerful.

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:46.120
Now, what do we want to do next?

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:48.470
Imagine we built this app.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:51.340
It's a great app, and we want
to share it with the world.

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:55.510
But we don't want to
give people the container

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:57.110
and tell them to
run a container.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:18:58.720
We want to just
deploy it on the web,

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:00.880
and have people be
able to visit a website

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:03.350
and experience the app.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:08.090
So we really want to send this
app or deploy it in the Cloud.

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:12.230
Now, one of the great
things about the product

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:16.010
I'm going to be using to deploy
it, which is called Cloud Run,

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:17.690
is that it's going
to give you all

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:19.850
of that administrative
stuff that I talked

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:22.620
about at the beginning--
all of that infrastructure--

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:24.980
it's going to give
you that for free.

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:29.300
It's going to give you very good
reliability and availability.

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:31.832
It's going to scale
automatically.

00:19:31.832 --> 00:19:34.040
It's going to minimize your
costs because you're only

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:37.280
going to pay for when the
app is actually running.

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:40.190
It's going to give you the
ability in one easy step

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:42.720
to add a custom domain name.

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:46.730
It's going to give you
security with standard SSL,

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:50.973
TLS, HTTPS type encryption.

00:19:50.973 --> 00:19:52.640
And as you're going
to see, it gives you

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:55.630
very good response time.

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:56.590
So how do we do that?

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.740
Well, let's go back
to the Cloud Editor.

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:02.670
Let's go to our main.py file.

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:06.880
And where it says name equals
world, instead of world,

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:10.380
we're going to say
from Cloud Run.

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.027
So when we run
this on Cloud Run,

00:20:12.027 --> 00:20:13.860
we should see a slightly
different message--

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:16.560
Hello from Cloud Run.

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:19.600
Now that we've made that
very small source change,

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:23.970
let's go ahead and run it.

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:26.910
Now, this lab says
to do a build/submit.

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:28.260
But we've already done that.

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:30.630
So we actually can
skip that step.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:32.800
And we should be able to
just run this command.

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:35.990
So let's go back
to the terminal.

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:40.070
Let me [INAUDIBLE]
this [INAUDIBLE] here.

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:41.900
And we're going to run
the deploy command.

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:45.140
Now what this deploy is
saying is, the name of my app

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:45.740
is Hello.

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:48.080
That's the dollar app
variable that we set.

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:50.550
The image I want you
to use is dollar tag.

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:53.840
We've set that as well and
been using that when we built.

00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:56.780
I want the managed
platform, which

00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:58.550
is-- there's another
way you can use

00:20:58.550 --> 00:21:06.680
Cloud Run, where you can
use your own lower-level

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:07.370
infrastructure.

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:09.800
But we're going to use the
higher-abstraction level

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:12.290
and request this
managed platform.

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:13.490
You can specify the region.

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:16.190
And Allow Unauthenticated--
this part right here--

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:17.780
is basically saying,
I want anybody

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:19.400
to be able to
access this website.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:21.290
They don't need to log in.

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:23.450
And you can see, I
talked a lot longer

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:25.290
than it took for it
to deploy this app.

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:27.320
It's actually all ready to go.

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:31.190
Now, we can't just do
the local preview here

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:32.960
because it's actually
been assigned

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:36.980
a globally accessible URL.

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:39.740
But we can just click
on this URL to see it.

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:41.630
And there's our app once again.

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:43.250
But now, it's
running in the Cloud.

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:47.120
So if we send a lot of traffic
to it-- and we will later--

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:49.250
we can see that it'll
automatically scale

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:53.330
to meet the offered demand.

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:55.280
So that's how easy
it was to take

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:58.850
an app that was in development,
to containerize it,

00:21:58.850 --> 00:22:01.720
and to run it on the Cloud.

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:05.960
Now, as easy as
that was, I still

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:09.230
had to think about this
notion of a Docker file,

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:12.860
and I had to come up
with that blueprint

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:21.590
in order to figure out how
to build the container image.

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:24.055
Wouldn't it be nice if I
didn't have to do that?

00:22:24.055 --> 00:22:25.430
And that's exactly
what I'm going

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:28.400
to show you how to do using
something called Buildpacks.

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:30.530
So I want you to go back
to the editor again.

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:34.160
And we're going to, once
again, change the message.

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:37.280
Oh, I forgot to point out
earlier that the message said

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:39.710
from Cloud Run, which showed
us we were actually getting

00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:41.750
that version of the app.

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:43.430
But now, we're
going to change it

00:22:43.430 --> 00:22:45.800
from saying from
Cloud Run to saying

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:48.920
from Cloud Run with Buildpacks.

00:22:48.920 --> 00:22:53.090
And in order to use Buildpacks,
we have to add one more file.

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:56.780
So under Hello, I'm
going to say New File.

00:22:56.780 --> 00:22:59.130
And we're going to create
a file called PROC file--

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:02.870
P-R-O-C-F-I-L-E
with a capital P.

00:23:02.870 --> 00:23:04.910
Go ahead and create that now.

00:23:04.910 --> 00:23:09.170
And all that has to be in
this file is this one line--

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:14.600
web, telling it's a web app,
python3 is the environment.

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:19.460
And starting up
the app is really

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:22.250
giving it the equivalent of
how do I start the container.

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:24.860
And it's simply
python3 main.py which

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:26.600
we've been using all along.

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:30.680
Now with this one line and a
slightly different deployment

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:34.460
command, I'm going to get the
system to build the Docker file

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:37.970
and create the image
for me without me

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:41.700
having to work out what the
Docker file should look like.

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:46.700
So what I'm going to do is
actually delete this file.

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:48.900
Now the Docker file's gone.

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:50.540
And if I go back
to the terminal,

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:55.080
and do an [? LX, ?] you can see
there's [? my croc ?] profile.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:56.970
But there's no
longer a Docker file.

00:23:56.970 --> 00:24:00.530
And so now I'm going to
build the same service

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:02.690
and let the system
figure out how

00:24:02.690 --> 00:24:04.983
to make the container for me.

00:24:04.983 --> 00:24:06.650
That's going to take
a little bit longer

00:24:06.650 --> 00:24:08.030
because it has to do a build.

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:10.590
But let me just explain
what's different.

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:13.310
Instead of giving
an image with a tag,

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:14.460
I didn't have to do that.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:18.830
You'll notice there's no dollar
tag appearing in this command.

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:21.860
But there's this new argument,
dash dash source dot.

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:24.740
And dot in Linux
and Unix derived

00:24:24.740 --> 00:24:28.230
systems is a shorthand
for the current directory.

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:32.480
So this is telling Gcloud, all
of a source for my app lives

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:34.740
right in this directory.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:36.660
And I didn't give you a tag.

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:39.210
But I put a [? croc ?]
file on there to tell you

00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:41.490
the minimum I need to tell you.

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:44.040
Create the container for me.

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:47.820
Build it, deposit it in
the Container Repository,

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:50.357
and deploy it--
all in one command.

00:24:50.357 --> 00:24:52.440
So now I've gotten to the
point where I don't even

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:54.240
have to think about
a Docker file, what

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:57.180
it is, how to code it--

00:24:57.180 --> 00:24:59.880
I can just, again,
create my app,

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:01.200
and deploy it to the Cloud.

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:05.860
It just makes everything
easier for me as a developer.

00:25:05.860 --> 00:25:07.690
As I said, this
takes a bit longer.

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:11.740
So what I might do is jump ahead
to the next step of the lab.

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:15.730
And we'll keep an eye on
this window in the meantime.

00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:21.510
So do you have to
use the command line?

00:25:21.510 --> 00:25:24.020
Obviously, I'm doing everything
in this lab via the command

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:24.520
line.

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:30.430
And I'm going to just
increase the size here

00:25:30.430 --> 00:25:32.620
so it's a little easier to see.

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:35.490
And the short answer
is, no, you don't

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:36.930
have to use the command line.

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:40.020
I used it in this lab
because it's easy to show.

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:42.300
And it's easy to
reproduce and copy things.

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:47.730
But there's three different
ways to use Google Cloud.

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:50.280
One is through the command
line that we've seen a lot of.

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:52.470
Another is through
the web interface,

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:55.980
which is up here at
the top of the screen.

00:25:55.980 --> 00:25:57.720
And the last is
programmatically.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:02.190
So you can use any of a number
of supported client libraries

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:03.880
as well.

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:05.610
So with that in
mind, I do just want

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:09.090
to show you quickly what
the web portion looks like.

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:12.430
By the way, this
finished building--

00:26:12.430 --> 00:26:13.480
I think, yes.

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:14.560
And it's now deploying.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:15.310
And it's done.

00:26:15.310 --> 00:26:18.130
So we get another URL here.

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:21.160
And it's not going to look too
much different, except that now

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:23.410
it says with Buildpacks.

00:26:23.410 --> 00:26:26.440
This is telling us it
got our new version.

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:29.620
So we were able to deploy
this version without even

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:31.180
thinking about a Docker file--

00:26:31.180 --> 00:26:34.860
straight to the Cloud with
all these nice benefits.

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:37.460
So what I wanted
to show you here

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:41.590
is what the web version
of all this looks like.

00:26:41.590 --> 00:26:44.930
So you have this
project up here.

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:49.580
And with the navigation
menu in the upper left,

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:54.530
you can go to the
Cloud Run page.

